from author_common import *
name='essmath_2_8'
items=[
item('E1','A club needs a workable budget','Illustrative school-club case. Funds available: 30 members contribute KES 200 each. Planned costs: venue KES 1200; 30 notebooks at KES 80 each; transport KES 1800; reserve KES 600. Compute income, each cost and the total. Is the plan affordable? Suggest one justified adjustment if needed.','Income 6000; notebooks 2400; costs 6000 including reserve, so balanced. Reserve is planned money kept available, not a fee already spent. Distinguish a budget from a record of actual spending. Use mixed-attainment pairs and large-print copies as needed.'),
item('E2','Discount offers','Illustrative quotations for identical notebooks, with equal quality and no delivery charges: Vendor P lists KES 80 each and offers 10% off; Vendor Q lists KES 75 each and offers 5% off. Find the saving and net price per notebook for each; compare the cost of 30. Extension: why is the larger percentage discount not enough to choose a vendor?','P saving 8, net 72, total 2160. Q saving 3.75, net 71.25, total 2137.50; Q cheaper by 22.50. Percentage base is the marked price. Do not round 3.75 to whole shillings before multiplying.'),
item('E3','Commission and percentage commission','Illustrative sales agreement: a salesperson earns 4% of KES 15000 in sales. Calculate commission. A second salesperson earns KES 450 on sales of KES 18000. Find the rate. For this exercise there is no salary, tax or other payment. Explain what changes if sales double and the rate stays fixed.','600; second rate 450/18000 × 100 = 2.5%. Double sales doubles commission under this explicitly proportional agreement. Commission is remuneration paid to the salesperson, not automatically a discount for the buyer.'),
item('E4','Profit and loss use cost as the base','Illustrative stall records: Batch A costs KES 2400 altogether and sells for KES 3000; Batch B costs KES 2000 and sells for KES 1700. Calculate profit or loss and its percentage of cost for each. The stated cost includes all costs for this task. Explain why dividing A profit by selling price answers a different question.','A profit 600, profit percentage 25%; B loss 300, loss percentage 15%. 600/3000 = 20% is profit as a fraction of revenue (margin), not percentage profit on cost. Margin is a contrast to prevent a denominator error, not an extra assessed outcome.'),
item('E5','Read a currency board from the dealer’s perspective','Illustrative rates, NOT current rates. Currency USD; dealer buys at KES 128 per USD; dealer sells at KES 132 per USD. No extra fees. (a) A visitor gives the dealer USD 50: how many KES? (b) A customer wants to buy USD 50: how many KES must they pay? Write the direction of each exchange before calculating.','(a) Dealer buys USD: 50×128=6400 KES. (b) Dealer sells USD: 50×132=6600 KES. Buying and selling labels always refer here to the dealer, not the learner.'),
item('E6','Two-way conversion and checking units','Use the E5 board, with no fees. (a) Convert KES 6600 into USD. (b) Convert KES 6400 received from selling USD 50 back into USD. Round a final currency amount to 2 decimal places. Is a round trip free? Show one unit check.','(a) 6600/132=50 USD. (b) 6400/132=48.4848… USD ≈48.48, loss ≈1.52 USD. Keep unrounded intermediate values. KES divided by KES/USD yields USD. The spread causes this loss; do not count it twice.'),
item('E7','Revise the club’s purchasing plan','Return to E1. Choose the cheaper E2 quotation for 30 notebooks. Replace only the notebook cost; keep venue 1200, transport 1800 and reserve 600. Compute revised total planned allocation and unallocated funds. A salesperson’s employer separately pays the E3 commission: should the club subtract it from its funds? Explain.','Total 1200+2137.50+1800+600=5737.50; unallocated 262.50. Commission paid by the employer is not an additional club expense in this scenario. Reserve of 600 is already inside the total; unallocated money is additional. Do not invent a tax or fee absent from the task.'),
item('E8','A fair quotation and consumer information card','In groups of four, prepare a one-page quotation for a fictional school club: include marked price, discount, quantity, all charges, final total, rate assumptions and expiry/date if relevant. Exchange cards with another group for checking. Write two sentences explaining why arithmetic and transparent assumptions help people make everyday decisions. No real buying, fundraising or posting is required.','Accept varied fictional quotations only if reproducible from the stated numbers. Check percentage bases, fees and disclosure of imaginary rates. Ethical communication and willingness to correct an error are observable evidence for outcome f; do not grade patriotism from a learner’s personal views.'),
item('E9','Final independent assessment data','All figures are illustrative. A club has KES 4000. It plans 20 books marked KES 120 each, with a 15% discount, plus transport KES 500 and reserve KES 300. Separate sales agreement: 3% commission on KES 8000 sales. A stall buys stock for KES 1600 and sells it for KES 2000. Dealer buys USD at KES 125 and sells USD at KES 130; no fees.','Assessment key appears in FinalExplanation_TeacherKey. Do not distribute this teacher version to learners during assessment. Expected: books 2040, allocation 2840, unallocated 1160; commission240; profit400=25%; sell USD20 yields2500KES; buying USD20 costs2600KES. Assessment has no current market-rate claim.')]
checks=[check('income','30*200',6000),check('budget','1200+30*80+1800+600',6000),check('P','30*80*(1-10/100)',2160),check('Q','30*75*(1-5/100)',2137.5),check('commission','15000*4/100',600),check('rate','450/18000*100',2.5),check('profit','(3000-2400)/2400*100',25),check('loss','(2000-1700)/2000*100',15),check('sell','50*128',6400),check('buy','50*132',6600),check('roundtrip','6400/132',48.4848484848,1e-8),check('newbudget','1200+2137.5+1800+600',5737.5),check('remaining','6000-5737.5',262.5),check('assessbooks','20*120*(1-15/100)',2040),check('assessbudget','2040+500+300',2840),check('assessleft','4000-2840',1160),check('assesscommission','8000*3/100',240),check('assessprofit','(2000-1600)/1600*100',25),check('assesssell','20*125',2500),check('assessbuy','20*130',2600)]
L=[]
L.append(lesson(1,'Can the club afford its plan?',['a','f'],['E1'],'Prepare and defend a balanced club budget.','Income, expenditure, reserve and unallocated funds have different meanings.','Organise a budget and verify its total.','Why do we need a budget?','The class meets a fictional club whose list of costs seems affordable. A budget becomes the shared decision model.',[
('Predict whether E1 is affordable and show a rough total.','Give 30 seconds silent thinking, then compare in pairs. Ask “Does reserve count in the plan?”','Every learner records a prediction, not just a yes/no vote.'),
('Complete E1 in pairs; identify income and each planned cost.','Allow 8 minutes working, 4 comparing with another pair and 4 collecting two explanations.','Check whether 30 notebooks becomes 2400 and reserve is included once.'),
('Explain why a balanced budget can include money not yet spent.','Build columns headed income, planned allocation and remaining funds. Ask two learners to defend their totals.','Each learner distinguishes reserve from an actual payment.'),
('Add questions about cheaper purchases to the class question board.','Keep “Can we save on notebooks?” open for Lesson 2.','Ask what evidence would answer the question.'),
('Draw the club’s first budget model with all values and a conclusion.','Retain this model for E7; invite a correction in a different colour.','Exit check: state income and total planned allocation, each 6000.')],['A 6000 KES income and 6000 KES allocation.','A budget plans the use of money and can include a reserve.','The club can fund this plan, with no extra unallocated funds.']))
L.append(lesson(2,'Which discount saves the most?',['b','f'],['E2'],'Compare net prices rather than discount percentages alone.','Discount is calculated on marked price; compare equivalent purchases.','Calculate discount, percentage discount and final costs.','Why are discounts useful in trading?','The club compares two quotations with different marked prices. The bigger discount percentage does not settle which is cheaper.',[
('Choose P or Q before calculating; give a reason.','Display the equal-quality/no-delivery assumptions from E2.','Record whether the initial reason uses price as well as percentage.'),
('Compute E2 individually, then compare net prices for 30.','Separate discount amount from net price; let pairs check with calculators after written setup.','Inspect the base used for 10% and 5%.'),
('Explain the cheaper offer using both totals.','Ask “What does 10% refer to?” and contrast 2160 with 2137.50.','An independent check recovers the stated net price from marked price minus discount.'),
('Mark the cheaper-purchase question answered with its assumptions.','Add “Could delivery change the decision?” as a conditional question.','Learners name the condition under which the comparison is valid.'),
('Add a quotation-comparison rule to the budget model.','Ask pairs to state the 22.50 saving from choosing Q rather than P.','Exit: recover the 5% rate from 3.75/75.')],['Different marked prices change the value of a discount.','Compare total net costs for the same quantity and conditions.','Q is cheaper here even though its percentage discount is smaller.']))
L.append(lesson(3,'Who receives commission?',['c','f'],['E3'],'Interpret and calculate a commission agreement.','Commission equals rate multiplied by the stated sales base.','Find both commission and an unknown percentage rate.','Why are commissions paid in trading?','The class distinguishes a salesperson’s payment from a buyer’s discount before introducing commission into everyday decisions.',[
('Identify who receives the payment in E3.','Write salesperson, employer and buyer on three paper slips before class, or assign these roles orally; no money changes hands.','Each pair names the payer and recipient.'),
('Solve both E3 cases and test the doubling claim.','Give pairs 10 minutes, then compare one multiplication and one division method.','Check 4% becomes 0.04 and the reverse rate is 2.5%.'),
('Explain how to recover a rate from the amount earned.','Write rate=commission/sales×100%; distinguish this sales base from marked-price discount.','Ask each learner to explain the denominator.'),
('Record when commission affects a budget and when it does not.','Keep the question of club liability open until E7.','Look for a need to read the agreement rather than assume.'),
('Add payer, recipient, base and rate to the decision model.','Use “If sales double…” as a final proportional reasoning check.','Exit: calculate 4% of 30000, with units.')],['A 4% payment on 15000 is 600.','The agreement determines the recipient, rate and base.','A club should count a commission only if it is liable to pay it.']))
L.append(lesson(4,'Profit and loss: percentage of what?',['d','f'],['E4'],'Calculate percentage profit and loss using cost price.','Profit or loss compares revenue with total cost; the percentage base here is cost.','Calculate differences and justify the denominator.','Can a correct division answer the wrong financial question?','A stall’s two batches reveal why naming the percentage base is necessary.',[
('Predict which batch makes a profit and which makes a loss.','Ask for signs before numerical percentages.','Each learner correctly labels the direction.'),
('Work E4 and annotate the numerator and denominator.','Pairs compare 600/2400 and 600/3000; label the different quantities without adding margin to assessment scope.','Check A25% and B15% loss.'),
('Explain percentage profit as a comparison with money invested.','Ask “What amount was at risk before the sale?”','A learner can explain why 20% is not the requested A percentage.'),
('Add a percentage-base check to the question board.','Connect with marked price and sales base from earlier lessons.','Learners name three contexts with their correct base.'),
('Extend the model with profit/loss and a base label.','Require units on the difference and a percent sign on the rate.','Exit: state A profit and its percentage separately.')],['The same 600 profit can form different fractions.','Percentage profit/loss is based on cost in this task.','Naming the base prevents a misleading trading claim.']))
L.append(lesson(5,'Read the exchange board',['e','f'],['E5'],'Choose a buying or selling rate from the dealer’s perspective.','The dealer buys foreign currency at one rate and sells at another.','Set up a currency conversion with units.','Whose buying and selling do the labels describe?','The club explores a fictional exchange board. The two rates refer to the dealer’s actions.',[
('Select a rate for each E5 case without computing.','Before class, write USD and KES on paper slips; let one learner play dealer and one visitor using these slips, or label the directions on the board.','Every learner states what the dealer receives.'),
('Solve E5 and annotate KES/USD on the rate.','Pairs perform a paper role-play; require a direction arrow before using a calculator.','Check 6400KES and6600KES, with no reversed rate.'),
('Explain why the customer pays more to buy the same USD amount.','Compare the two explicitly fictional rates; state that no fee is included.','Learner explains both rate choice and multiplication.'),
('Add the reverse-conversion question for Lesson 6.','Ask “If we start with KES, what operation reverses this?”','Collect predictions to revisit.'),
('Add a dealer-perspective arrow to the model.','Keep the E5 board available next lesson.','Exit: choose the correct rate when selling USD to the dealer.')],['USD50 produces6400KES when sold and costs6600KES when bought.','Choose the rate by the dealer’s action.','Transparent rate labels support a correct financial decision.']))
L.append(lesson(6,'Reverse conversion and the round trip',['e','f'],['E5','E6'],'Convert from KES and account for the exchange spread once.','Reverse conversion divides by the chosen KES-per-USD rate.','Use units and round only the final monetary answer.','Why does changing currency twice reduce the starting amount?','The earlier board now tests whether reversing a calculation undoes the economic cost of exchanging currency.',[
('Predict whether USD50 returns as USD50.','Retain predictions; point out the two unequal rates.','Each prediction includes a reason.'),
('Calculate both E6 conversions and check units.','Give 10 minutes working and6 peer comparison; calculators may retain all intermediate digits.','Check 50USD and48.48USD.'),
('Explain the approximately1.52USD loss.','Trace one full round trip; do not charge a separate imaginary fee.','Ask a learner to identify where the spread enters the calculation.'),
('Close the reverse-conversion question with both examples.','Discuss why current rates would require a dated source, while these are labelled illustrative.','A learner identifies the limitation of the example.'),
('Add rounding and unit checks to the model.','Require one calculation without a calculator to verify order of magnitude.','Exit: show KES/(KES/USD)=USD.')],['A round trip returns approximately48.48USD from50USD.','Use the correct rate and divide for reverse conversion.','The spread reduces the amount even without extra fees.']))
L.append(lesson(7,'Defend a revised club budget',['a','b','c','f'],['E1','E2','E3','E7'],'Integrate earlier results into a transparent purchasing decision.','Avoid double-counting reserve or costs paid by another party.','Revise a budget and defend assumptions.','Which changes belong in the club budget?','Learners return to the original club decision and incorporate the cheaper quotation without importing unrelated costs.',[
('Predict the effect of Q on unallocated funds.','Ask learners to retrieve E1 and E2 rather than trust memory.','Check that an expected saving is positive.'),
('Complete E7 and annotate which items changed.','Assign reader, calculator, checker and reporter; rotate roles.','Observe whether commission is added without a liability.'),
('Defend the5737.50 total and262.50 unallocated funds.','Ask “Where is the600 reserve?” and request a reconciliation to6000.','Require allocation plus unallocated funds to equal income.'),
('Review whether the original driving question is answered.','Record any conditions that would require a new quotation.','Learners distinguish a conclusion from a condition.'),
('Produce the final budget model with assumptions visible.','Allow pairs to challenge one another’s accounting.','Exit: explain why the employer-paid commission is excluded.')],['Replacing one quotation releases262.50KES.','A budget changes only when its own income or liabilities change.','The club can defend the cheaper plan with a complete reconciliation.']))
L.append(lesson(8,'Communicate a fair financial decision',['a','b','f'],['E8'],'Communicate and check a reproducible everyday financial claim.','A quotation needs a base, quantity, conditions and total.','Construct and independently check a consumer information card.','How can clear arithmetic protect a customer?','Pairs use the decision model to create and check a fictional quotation. This is the school-based community-awareness task.',[
('Identify a misleading offer that omits its base price.','Use a verbal example with no named real trader.','Learners name the missing information.'),
('Create E8 cards and exchange them for independent checking.','Allow8 minutes creation,6 checking and2 returning corrections; use simple invented numbers.','Reviewer can reproduce the final total from the card.'),
('Explain one correction and its consequence for a customer.','Ask for a mathematical reason and a communication improvement.','Assess outcome f through the written justification.'),
('Mark remaining questions for teacher follow-up.','Keep personal income and family spending out of discussion.','All unresolved questions have an owner or next step.'),
('Compile a five-check financial decision guide.','Use price base, rate direction, units, total and assumptions as prompts.','Collect each learner’s two-sentence everyday-use explanation.')],['A transparent quotation can be recalculated by another person.','Correct arithmetic needs explicit assumptions.','The club decision can be explained honestly to others.']))
L.append(lesson(9,'Independent application and reflection',['a','b','c','d','e','f'],['E9'],'Demonstrate the six outcomes independently.','Apply the unit’s financial rules to a fresh illustrative case.','Select methods, calculate accurately and justify a choice.','Can another person reproduce my decision?','Learners apply the established methods independently. The teacher records evidence before any feedback.',[
('Read the task and identify required outputs.','Give E9 and the student Final Explanation only; allow questions about wording.','Confirm that learners can see the data and know rounding rules.'),
('Complete all five assessment sections independently.','Allow30 minutes; provide a basic calculator, no worked answers. Do not open optional ARES resources or search links until original assessment work is collected. Read wording aloud as an access adjustment without choosing methods.','Collect original work for outcome-by-outcome marking.'),
('Write one sentence about the check used, without changing collected work.','Give2 minutes private reflection; postpone worked solutions until marking.','Record a checking strategy separately from correctness.'),
('Submit one question for later feedback.','Use1 minute to collect questions.','Identify which methods need reteaching.'),
('State one everyday decision the unit helps explain.','Use3 minutes for individual transfer reflections; no public scores.','Outcome f is supported by the final reflection and E8.')],['Independent work provides evidence of all six outcomes.','Methods must match the financial quantity and its base.','A defensible decision combines calculation with a clear explanation.'],minutes=[4,30,2,1,3],independent=True))
m=dict(subject='Essential Mathematics',substrand_id='2.8',topic='Commercial Arithmetic 1',folder='Essential_Mathematics',prefix='Codex_Essential_Mathematics_Commercial_Arithmetic_1',unit=dict(strand='2.0 Measurements and Geometry',substrand='Sub-Strand 2.8: Commercial Arithmetic (1)',content='Budget; Discounts, and commission; Profit and loss; Conversion of different currencies',coreCompetencies='Citizenship; Critical thinking and problem solving. Source descriptions retained in the curriculum evidence; activities involve interpreting rate tables and checking shopping decisions.',values='Peace; Patriotism. Learners listen respectfully and communicate honestly about transactions in Kenya Shillings.',pcis='Financial literacy.',sep='Mathematical modelling, proportional reasoning and verification.',careers='Bookkeeping, retail, sales and financial administration.',focus='Prepare, calculate and justify everyday financial decisions with explicit percentage bases and exchange-rate direction.',phenomenon='A fictional school club can balance its first budget but wants to know whether advertised discounts and exchange offers improve its position.',supportingPhenomena='Illustrative quotations, sales agreements and a dealer exchange board. Figures are not real market prices or current rates.',drivingQuestion='How can a school club make and defend an affordable financial decision? KICD: Why do we need a budget? Why are discounts and commissions necessary in trading activities?',storylineThread='Budget -> discounts -> commission -> profit/loss -> currency direction -> reverse conversion -> integrated decision -> communication -> independent assessment.'),lessons=L,assessment=dict(subjectLabel='COMMERCIAL ARITHMETIC (1)',instructions='Time:30 minutes within Lesson9. Work independently using E9. A basic calculator is allowed. Show the percentage base and currency direction. Round final monetary amounts to2 decimal places where needed. All data are illustrative; no taxes or additional fees apply.',sections=[dict(title='1. Budget and discount',prompt='Use E9. Calculate the discounted cost of20 books, total planned allocation including transport and reserve, and unallocated funds. Explain why the reserve is included.',exemplar='Discount per book=120×0.15=18KES; net102KES.20 books=2040KES. Total=2040+500+300=2840KES. Unallocated=4000−2840=1160KES. Reserve is planned money held for contingencies, so it is already part of the allocation.'),dict(title='2. Commission',prompt='Calculate the salesperson’s commission in E9. State the base used.',exemplar='3% of8000KES=240KES; base is total sales of8000KES.'),dict(title='3. Percentage profit',prompt='Calculate the stall’s profit and its percentage profit on cost.',exemplar='Profit=2000−1600=400KES; percentage=400/1600×100=25%.'),dict(title='4. Currency directions',prompt='Using E9, calculate(a) KES received when selling USD20 to the dealer and(b) KES needed to buy USD20. Explain which rate applies in each case.',exemplar='(a) Dealer buys USD:20×125=2500KES.(b) Dealer sells USD:20×130=2600KES. The labels describe the dealer’s actions.'),dict(title='5. Explain a decision',prompt='State one practical benefit of preparing a budget and one detail you would check before accepting a real quotation or exchange offer.',exemplar='A budget tests affordability and allocates limited funds. Check an applicable detail such as quantity, marked price, fees, date or the dealer’s buying/selling direction. Accept another justified practical example.')],rubric=rubric(['Prepare a usable budget and calculate discounted costs','Calculate commission and percentage profit using the correct base','Convert currencies using the dealer’s stated rates','Explain and communicate a financial decision'])))
